From: fnerd@smds.com (FutureNerd Steve Witham)
Date: Mon, 14 Feb 94 16:51:06 EST
Subject: [#94-2-140] PCR

Tim Starr = >, fnerd (me) = > >

> >I think it only invites infinite regress if someone's inclined to it.
> 
> False.  The logical implication exists regardless of whether anyone's ever
> inclined to it.

I don't know exactly what "implication" you mean.  I thought you were saying
that the question of "why do you believe that" led to an infinite regress--
which it does, if you want to dig in that direction.  PCR says don't ask why.
How do you avoid this sort of infinite regress--what do you do about the
question, "Why do you believe that...and why do you believe that?" etc.?

> But PCR seems to say that you're not allowed to demand justification, and
> on what grounds?

PCR doesn't *do* things on grounds.  It's just asserted, and invites
criticism on any basis *but* "on what grounds."

> The positive case is the case for PCR, as opposed to the case against
> justificationism.  As a matter of fact, I'm not at all sure there is one
> at all, nor that there needs to be - this is one of my running criticisms
> of PCR!

Justificationism is the name Bartley coined for the problem PCR addresses.
If you either don't think it's a problem or you have a better solution, then
PCR doesn't do you any good.  I think he's got a point and I don't know 
whether you think he doesn't, or you think it's not *much* of a point, or
what.

> >...there's 
> >something fishy about an argument that goes "This is true because anyone who
> >gets into an argument about it with me is going to lose."
> 
> Disanalogy.  A better analogy would be to someone who claims that you can win
> a swordfight without swords, slashing, etc.

I think yours is an analogy to what you argue *against*?

> But putting it in terms of a two-
> party contest confuses things.  The real point is that argumentation, in the
> sense of figuring out how things in the world work, is impossible without
> some axioms, because argumentation presupposes them.

The PCR model, which I like, is more like, you enter an argument with *all*
you believe, but willing to hold *any* belief suspended.  There are no axioms
that are always held onto.  (Maybe they're implicit but not known for certain.)

I understand that by "presupposes," you mean there are things that are part of
the nature of argument.  I'm sure argument has a nature, but I'm not sure
that I know what it is, nor that there are any characteristics that are in 
common between all the useful things that could arguably be called arguments.

Do axioms have some special status because they're related to the nature of
argumentation?  If so, what?  If not, why do they have a special term?

> >(Hmm.  Paragraph stops there.)  All decision requires is acting one way more
> >than another in one instance.  You don't have to know what you believe.
> 
> Yes, you do, otherwise you couldn't act.  Action, being purposeful behavior,
> must be guided by some believed purpose.  If you don't know what you believe
> your purpose is, then you can't behave accordingly.  Thought is a necessary
> condition for action.

So why is "action" better or more interesting than pure physical,
biological, reflexive or unconscious behavior?  You seem to be implying that
if something's not justified, it's not action.  But if I assert that nothing's
justified, and that means I'm not a purposeful actor in your book, then that's
okay with me.  (A little funny, but I assume you have something to teach me...)

> >To me it seems more apt to say that in one sense I can't know.  I can't know,
> >and know I know, and know I know I know...forever.
> 
> What sense?  One of omnipotent infallibility?  Which is impossible?  Why
> speak of not meeting an impossible standard?

*That* sense...that if "knowing" means you also know the basis for knowing,
etc., then it's impossible.  So I use "know" and "believe" more loosely,
sort of behavioristically--"inclined to behave as if."  

> Mystics left us a standard of truth that it must be revealed to us from
> some omniscient, infallibile source - an impossible standard.  But, rather
> than rejecting the standard and replacing it with a better one, all too
> many have rejected the possibility that truth can be known.  PCR seems to
> follow this path.  

PCR has a concept of a surviving belief (belief in a looser sense than yours, 
maybe like mine).  What's your standard of truth?

> I never said that idea-generating processes WERE theories of knowledge, I
> said that they depended upon theories of knowledge.  This because generating
> an idea is a kind of human action, and human actions must be guided by
> some idea.

Okay, I disagree, but I think this is partly definitional.  If idea-generation
depends on theories of knowlege, what do theories of knowlege depend on?
Eventually everything comes out of pure physical, unconscious processes, 
right?  What are the steps, so I can have an idea where your distinctions are?

> >  So when *do* you need 
> >an actual theory of knowledge, actual ideas about it?
> 
> When you're actually knowing anything.

Once again I disagree.  (To me knowing something and knowing how or why or 
on what basis you know it, are two completely separate things.)  But I think
this is definitional too--you seem to include having a theory of knowledge
in the definition of what's truly knowledge.  Or maybe you're saying that,
say, unconscious life forms *embody* a theory of knowledge without actually
knowing it consciously?  Does an ant know how to walk?  Do you have a 
category for "tacit knowlege?"

