Subject: [#94-2-144] Bounded Random Walks (was Nanarchy)

Paul Cisek writes:
>I don't understand how bounded random walks apply to wealth or
>territory distribution.  First of all, what does it mean for all the
>points to pile up at the boundary - what is the space?

Consider vectors whose components are the fraction of wealth/territory
held by each competitor.  This is a "simplex" whose corners each have
one competitor holding everything.

>The key point is that wealth/power/territory distribution is not a
>random walk - what you have affects your ability to get more.  I try
>to think of this as a system of differential equations, where each
>variable grows as a function of itself, and shrinks as a function of
>the other variables, and the total sum is conserved (i.e. system is
>bounded).  If the growth/shrink function is a linear function of
>wealth, then original wealth distributions will be conserved.  If it
>is faster-than-linear (ex. x^2), then a single winner emerges.  If it
>is slower-than-linear (ex. sqrt(x)), then the final state is uniform.
>Other functions have more interesting behaviors, like several winners
>achieving a stable balance.

Yes, you can have biased random walks, where the average delta step is
not zero.  With enough bias away from the boundary, the distribution
need not go to the boundary.

>Anyway, the point is that in the take-over scenario, the transfer
>function is primarily faster-than-linear, perhaps only reaching a
>plateau after all the (supposedly) limited wealth/power/territory has
>been subsumed.  Hence, "winner-takes-all".

You seem to claim bigger "goo" has an advantage, more than big
nations, companies, etc.  This claim is not obvious to me.  It needs
arguments offered in its favor.

Steve Witham writes:
>Robin Hanson argues that my goo war scenario is too general: ...
>Two more specifics then.  One is the idea that this situation would
>come about very suddenly, in historical terms.  

I don't yet see the relevance of this point.  How does the rate of
change affect the direction changes move toward?

>The other is that the goo would be simple stuff, not subject to much
>interesting internal politics, for instance.  Of course that
>contradicts somewhat its ability to wage war.  Also, "the competing
>entities" means the goo's owners, if we're to stick to the kind of
>situation that all-encompasing-libertarian blue goo is supposed to
>handle, rather than the simpler problem of stamping out independent
>grey goo.

Because of interstellar gas, there are economies of scale for fast
intersellar spacecraft - I recall Drexler working on a nanodesign of
about a meter in length.  Smaller craft get fried.  Because such a
craft has room for a nano-upload, and because intelligence is
valuable, I'd expect the smallest colonizing entities to be
intelligent.  Interstellar goo need *not* be dumb.

Anyway, sounds like you are not clear what scenario you want to argue
for.  The ball is in *your* court to describe your scenario and argue
why it should imply a winner-take-all, contrary to the usual result in
analogous contexts.  I can not respond to every possible argument you
might make.

Robin Hanson


