From: pavel@PARK.BU.EDU (Paul Cisek)
Date: Thu, 17 Feb 94 11:00:29 -0500
Subject: [#94-2-154] Bounded Random Walks (was Nanarchy)

>From: hanson@hss.caltech.edu (Robin Hanson)
>X-Message-Number: #94-2-144
>
>Consider vectors whose components are the fraction of wealth/territory
>held by each competitor.  This is a "simplex" whose corners each have
>one competitor holding everything.

I assume state space is bounded by an n-simplex rather than an n-hypercube
because of conservation of wealth/territory.  This is what I was describing
as a system of differential equations.  But the system cannot be understood
as an unbiased random walk, even if the result of such a model agrees with your
intuition about the problem.  It's important to consider the interaction
function between competing entities.  In a probabilistic scenario the
qualitative outcome of competition is determined by the nature of the
interaction function.  If, for example, your ability to expand is
logarithmic with your state of expansion, then the system will tend toward
uniformity, with random fluctuations never taking it far from this
equilibrium.

Of course, the reality is much more complicated.  The geometry of the space of
competition (I don't mean the state-space) also has a great effect on the
dynamics.  My discussion is of a dimensionless space, where "territory" is
merely the percentage of available wealth units owned.  A very important issue
is what wealth units are owned.  Are they all clumped together geographically?
Are they distributed?  Does competition only occur at region boundaries?

>You seem to claim bigger "goo" has an advantage, more than big
>nations, companies, etc.  This claim is not obvious to me.  It needs
>arguments offered in its favor.
>

Actually, my point is more a mathematical one.  I don't claim to have any
special knowledge into the nature of "goo" interactions.  It just seems
reasonable that an entity composed of cooperating units will have 
(exponentially) more power to expand as it expands.  But it could be
otherwise...  My claim is that a random walk model will not explain the
dynamics of a competitive system as long as there is even the slightest
interaction between competing entities.

Paul


