From: sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk (Stephen J. Whitrow)
Date: Thu, 17 Feb 94 20:31:46 GMT
Subject: [#94-2-156] PCR: Creation Ex Nihilo

Steve Witham writes:
>Reilly Jones speaks of many things.  i nitpick-
>
>> What about the axiom "existence exists"?  I have faith in this.  If PCR says we
>> should doubt this, then PCR could not be rational itself.
>
>I think existence makes the most sense in terms of a reference frame.  So
>I exist--in the reference frame of this universe--but Mickey Mouse doesn't.
>To say something exists by default means in the universe of the speaker.
>So to say that this universe, or existence itself, exists, seems incompletely
>specified.  You must not mean within this universe.

Yes, universes exist as far as their occupants are concerned, but their
existence does not go any further than this.  If the axiom "existence
exists" is taken to refer to some ultra-objective sense of existence, it
becomes false.

In Many Worlds / Many Minds, all possible permutations of reality exist in
some sense, in a superposition.  But in exactly what sense?  What if some
independent, external superbeing has to pass judgement on whether any of
these universes "really" exist?  We'll assume the learned superbeing
inhabits its own hyperspace which contains our universe(s), for instance,
thereby explaining why its spacetime happens to be external to ours, and
allowing its instruments and senses to bridge the gap between realities.

The superbeing makes the observations, and, lo and behold, concludes none
of these other universes exist.  They are merely mirages in the minds of
their resident lifeforms.  Each matter universe is cancelled out by its
antimatter polar opposite, which exists in a superposition.  And the mass
energy of each universe is cancelled out by negative gravitational energy.

But from the viewpoint of dwellers of these universes, things are quite
different.  A given individual will appear to exist in a multiverse -- a
myriad of universes where the individual's duplicates are superposed at
the same point in space and time.  The duplicates are compelled to share
consciousness, unless we postulate the existence of "spirits", since a
distinction between material duplicates would have to involve a non-
material substance.

Fortunately for each individual, its antimatter twin(s) exist(s) in a
different part of "reality space", which is totally inaccessible.  It
appears that polarity reversal is sufficient to cause separation of
consciousness into distinct multiverses, otherwise opposites could never
avoid cancelling each other out.  When the brains are sufficiently
distinct as to disallow merging of identity, consciousness sharing
becomes impossible, or requires telepathic powers.

The trouble with immortal universe cosmologies is that you've either got
to throw out the Second Law, or life is not possible.  Arthur Eddington
warned that: "...if your theory is shown to be against the Second Law of
Thermodynamics, I can give you no hope: there is nothing for it but to
collapse in deepest humiliation."  Luckily our world has less than
infinite entropy, but such universes must be finite.  And for those
of us who aren't entropic death-worshippers, any apparent eschatological
implications of finite universes are readily refuted.  As Mike Price has
demonstrated, there are ways and means around that sort of thing.

Maybe there are universes of infinite age and entropy, but they certainly
can't contain lifeforms.  And other finite universes, also lifeless, again
have no observers to confirm their existence.  They may be considered to
exist somewhere in reality space as theoretical possible permutations, but
have a zero reality coefficient, unlike life-bearing permutations.

So things work out pretty well for everyone.  The superbeing is not left
to ponder the question of how universes may suddenly pop into existence
ex-nihilo.  (It knows that its own hyperspace has always been annulled
by an antimatter version.)  And each occupant of the universes gets to
live in what hir peers generally agree is a quite objective reality, with
no-one in danger of being annihilated by an antimatter twin, or suddenly
getting voted out of existence due to unpopularity with the herd.  Well,
they might if they're just a simulation, or a brain in a vat.

